## Root Cause

At `List.tsx:42`, the code does `items.map(...)` where `items` is `undefined`. In React, this happens when the parent passes no `items` prop (or passes it conditionally as `undefined`). `undefined` has no `.map()` method, so it throws `TypeError`.

**Minimal reproduction:**
```tsx
// List.tsx — BROKEN
function List({ items }) {
  return <ul>{items.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>;
}
// <List />  ←  items is undefined → crash at line 42
```

## The Fix

**Pick one, applied at `List.tsx:42`:**

### 1. Default parameter (cleanest for a required array prop)
```tsx
function List({ items = [] }) {
  return <ul>{items.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

### 2. Nullish coalescing (inline, no signature change)
```tsx
function List({ items }) {
  return <ul>{(items ?? []).map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

### 3. Guard clause (most explicit, also handles non-array values)
```tsx
function List({ items }) {
  if (!Array.isArray(items)) return null;
  return <ul>{items.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

All three were verified above — each returns `[]` or `null` instead of throwing when `items` is `undefined`.

**Recommendation:** Use **Fix 1** (`= []`) if `items` is always supposed to be an array — it makes the contract explicit at the destructure site. Use **Fix 3** if you want defensive rendering that also handles `null` or malformed props gracefully. Avoid **Fix 2** as a habit — it's a one-liner but hides the missing-prop problem rather than making the intent clear.

Reproduced. `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')` is the V8/Node equivalent of React's `cannot read property 'map' of undefined`. Same root cause, same fix.

## Cause

`List.tsx:42` calls `items.map(...)`, but `items` is `undefined` — the caller didn't pass the prop (or passed `undefined` explicitly).

```tsx
// List.tsx — broken
function List({ items }: { items: number[] }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => <li key={item}>{item}</li>)}  {/* line 42: items is undefined */}
    </ul>
  );
}

// Caller omits the prop
<List />   // items === undefined → TypeError at render
```

## Fix (three layers, pick what fits)

**1. Guard at the call site — safest, one-line, always apply:**

```tsx
{(items ?? []).map(item => <li key={item}>{item}</li>)}
```

**2. Default the prop — clean when "empty list" is a valid state:**

```tsx
function List({ items = [] }: { items?: number[] }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => <li key={item}>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

**3. Make the prop required and fix the caller — when the component is meaningless without data:**

```tsx
function List({ items }: { items: number[] }) { ... }

// Caller must supply it:
<List items={data ?? []} />
```

**Which to pick:**

| Situation | Fix |
|---|---|
| Component should render nothing/empty when no data | **#2** (default prop) + keep **#1** as belt-and-suspenders |
| Caller is the real bug (should always pass data) | **#3** — keep prop required, fix the callsite |
| Quick hotfix, can't audit all callers now | **#1** — the `?? []` guard, right at line 42 |

The **root fix is #3** (the caller is violating the contract), but **#1 or #2 should be applied regardless** so the component degrades gracefully instead of crashing the render. A bare `.map` on a possibly-absent array is a latent crash; guarding it is not optional even after fixing the caller.
